\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Graph and orthogonal stabilizers}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{The stabilizers also distinguish the two notions. For the
[alternating four-cycle family](fdip-000X), with weights
#{(a,b,a,b)}, #{m>0}, and #{R=\{0,2\}}, the stabilizer of
#{(Q,P_R)} in #{O(4)} is #{(C_2)^2} if #{a\ne b}, and
#{(C_2)^3} if #{a=b}. The signed graph stabilizers are respectively
#{C_2} and #{C_2\times C_2}. By contrast, the orthogonal stabilizer
of #{Q} alone is continuous when #{a=b}; it contains #{O(2)} on
the repeated eigenspace.}

\proof{
\p{Use the orthonormal eigenvectors
##{\begin{aligned}
u_0&=(1,1,1,1)/2,&u_3&=(1,-1,1,-1)/2,\\
u_1&=(1,1,-1,-1)/2,&u_2&=(1,-1,-1,1)/2.
\end{aligned}}
Their eigenvalues are #{m^2,m^2+2a+2b,m^2+2b,m^2+2a},
respectively. In the ordered pairs #{(u_0,u_3)} and #{(u_1,u_2)},
#{P_R} has the same block
#{\frac12\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&1\end{pmatrix}}.
If #{a\ne b}, all four eigenvalues are distinct, so an orthogonal
commuter of #{Q} chooses one sign on each eigenline. Commuting with
#{P_R} makes the signs equal within each pair, leaving two independent
signs. If #{a=b}, the first pair still shares one sign. On
#{\operatorname{span}(u_1,u_2)}, an orthogonal commuter must preserve
the two lines spanned by #{u_1+u_2} and #{u_1-u_2}, leaving two
further signs. This proves the orthogonal counts.}

\p{For a signed graph symmetry #{r^ks^\epsilon} with
#{r(i)=i+1}, #{s(i)=-i}, the opposite set is preserved iff #{k}
is even. When #{a\ne b}, weight preservation additionally requires
#{k+\epsilon} even. Thus only #{1,r^2} remain; when #{a=b},
#{1,r^2,s,r^2s} remain. These are all possibilities because the
underlying simple-cycle automorphism group is #{D_4}, as in
\ref{fdip-000X}.}
}
